\subsection{Layer Effect (C3)}

Specification-layer types trend higher than linguistic-layer types (44.0\% vs.\ 37.9\%, $p = 0.017$), but C3 is inconclusive: the effect does not survive Holm--Bonferroni correction, controlling for token delta reverses the direction, and cross-layer type overlap (conditional precedence 36.8\% vs.\ scopal 39.4\%) further undermines the distinction. Type-level distinctions (C2) hold independently of layer grouping. We accordingly treat the two-layer structure as organizational convenience rather than a theoretical claim.

\subsection{Model $\times$ Type Interaction (C4) \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:c4}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and ambiguity type under ambiguous conditions ($n = 2{,}997$). Models judged by GPT-5.4: Claude, GPT-4.1, Qwen3; by GPT-4.1: GPT-5.4, DeepSeek. Authorization scope varies 52\,pp across models (upper bound; $\kappa = 0.385$), with within-judge-group ranges of 34\,pp (GPT-5.4-judged) vs.\ 4\,pp (GPT-4.1-judged). Incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%). (C4 interaction is authorization-scope-specific; excluding it yields $p = 0.97$.)}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

A model main effect is significant ($\chi^2 = 31.02$, $p = 3.0 \times 10^{-6}$, $V = 0.102$), with Claude (30.3\%) substantially less vulnerable than DeepSeek (49.7\%). Model identity explains only 1.0\% of total violation variance; the significant interaction ($F(20, 2976) = 1.97$, $p = 0.006$; $\mathrm{df} = (5{-}1) \times (6{-}1) = 20$; 2.9\% of variance; confirmed by logistic regression likelihood ratio test: $\chi^2(20) = 47.20$, $p = 5.5 \times 10^{-4}$) concentrates in specific type--model combinations.

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} highlights three suggestive patterns: (1) authorization scope is the \emph{most model-differentiating type} (52 pp range; Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), though this type has the lowest judge agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$; \S\ref{sec:limitations}), so absolute rates carry more uncertainty than for other types; (2) incompleteness is a \emph{consistent vulnerability} (46--62\%, 16 pp range); (3) conditional precedence is the most \emph{model-invariant type} (34--40\%, 6 pp range). The authorization scope ranking (Claude $<$ Qwen3 $<$ GPT-4.1) is stable within the GPT-5.4 judge subset (bootstrap ranking preservation: 99.5\%, $B = 10{,}000$; pairwise orderings $\geq$99.5\%), controlling for judge identity. The 34\,pp within-judge range (GPT-5.4-judged subset) far exceeds measurement noise, but given the low cross-judge $\kappa$, we characterize this pattern as suggestive rather than definitive. Both judges belong to the OpenAI model family; cross-family evaluation (e.g., Claude as judge) would provide independent confirmation. (The low $\kappa$ partly reflects the prevalence paradox: prevalence-adjusted $\kappa$ would be substantially higher; cf.\ the analogous gap in failure-mode coding, \S\ref{sec:limitations}.) Excluding authorization scope, the residual interaction vanishes ($p = 0.97$; Appendix~\ref{app:c4_sans_auth}), confirming that the C4 interaction concentrates in this single type.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Scope.} We evaluate seven API-accessible models from six providers (Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); smaller open-source models (7B--70B) may exhibit different vulnerability patterns. $\tau^2$-bench covers retail and airline customer service only---effects in other domains remain untested, though within-study cross-domain consistency is encouraging. Our matched-pair design requires synthetic ambiguity construction, which may not capture the full distribution of naturally occurring ambiguities; realism validation (\S\ref{sec:method}) confirms lexical comparability but cannot rule out distributional gaps. Coreferential ambiguity was excluded from mitigation and replication experiments due to benchmark-specific structural constraints (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}).

\paragraph{Measurement.} Each model is evaluated by a single cross-judge, confounding judge identity with model identity; within-judge-group analyses confirm C1 individually (both $p < 0.001$) but not C3 (Appendix~\ref{app:judge_group_ranking}), and cross-model type ranking concordance is only moderate (Appendix~\ref{app:kendall_w}). Most critically, cross-judge-group type rankings show near-zero correlation ($\rho = -0.03$; Appendix~\ref{app:judge_group_ranking}), meaning that the C2 gradient's mid-tier ordering does not generalize across judge groups---though incompleteness as the highest-risk type is robust to this variation. Per-type reliability varies---authorization scope has the lowest agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$) and per-type human--LLM estimates rest on small cell sizes with wide confidence intervals (Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement})---while failure mode coding achieves moderate agreement ($\kappa = 0.52$), with minority categories less reliable. Both judges belong to the OpenAI model family, so systematic leniency toward same-family outputs or severity toward competitors cannot be ruled out; cross-family judge evaluation (e.g., Claude judging GPT traces) would strengthen C4 conclusions. ICC is computed within the OpenAI model family (GPT-5.4 across runs); run-to-run variance for non-OpenAI models remains unmeasured.

\paragraph{Confounds.} Incompleteness resolution necessarily adds policy content, creating an information asymmetry; our logistic regression controls and coreferential lower-bound analysis mitigate but do not eliminate this confound (\S\ref{sec:results}). Our primary analysis uses a moderate-or-above severity threshold; switching to any-violation yields consistent results, with all four claims remaining significant and concordant type rankings.

\section*{Data and Code Availability}
The 300 clause pairs, evaluation pipeline, analysis scripts, and raw episode traces will be released upon publication at \url{https://github.com/anonymous/policy-ambiguity-safety}.

